TI  - Industrial Intelligence Systems: A Structured Review of Key Technologies, System Dependencies, and Engineering Trustworthiness
AU  - Li Zhu
AU  - Bin Li
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Industrial Intelligence
DO  - 10.56578/jii040204
AB  - Industrial intelligence relies on the integration of industrial software, interconnected production systems, digital twins, and artificial intelligence (AI) to support data-driven monitoring, predictive analysis, and operational decision-making.Despite advances in these technologies, differences in technological maturity, system interoperability, and engineering trustworthiness remain obstacles to their coordinated deployment.This study investigates the relationships among the principal technologies underlying industrial intelligence, with particular attention to their functional dependencies, implementation constraints, and capacity to support reliable industrial operations.A structured literature review was conducted using a corpus comprising 55 peer-reviewed publications and 29 supplementary technical and institutional sources.Industrial software, the industrial Internet, digital twins and digital threads, and industrial AI were examined through a common framework covering seven dimensions of technological and engineering readiness.The comparative analysis identified two recurring categories of deployment constraints.Industrial software and the industrial Internet were primarily constrained by the accumulation of domain-specific knowledge, legacy-system compatibility, and heterogeneous data interoperability.For digital twins and industrial AI, the review identified additional challenges concerning model verification, uncertainty assessment, and the integration of intelligent decisions into industrial processes.The analysis further identified interdependent requirements for data consistency, mechanism-based modeling, computational resources, and closed-loop decision execution.These findings indicate that the engineering readiness of industrial intelligence depends not only on the maturity of individual technologies but also on the reliability of their interactions within integrated production systems.The study establishes a system-level perspective for assessing industrial intelligence technologies and provides a structured basis for identifying integration bottlenecks, evaluating engineering trustworthiness, and directing research toward verifiable industrial decision-making.
