TY  - JOUR
TI  - Balancing Productivity and Flexibility in Lean Manufacturing for Complex Production Systems
AU  - Sinan Apak
AU  - Mehmet Gurel Tekelioglu
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Industrial Informatics
DO  - 10.1109/tii.2026.3720381
AB  - As literature suggests, there is usually a tradeoff between two performance measures, namely, productivity and flexibility, in complex lean manufacturing settings where productivity requires standardization and stability while flexibility demands adaptable systems. In this direction, this research examines the relationship between lean manufacturing strategies, aiming at providing sustainable and resilient solutions in increasing productivity and easing flexibility in complex production environments. More specifically, this article develops a hybrid methodological approach that combines multicriteria decision analysis Technique for Order of Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS), discrete event simulation (DES), and Bayesian inference. The methodology is tested on the quantitative and qualitative datasets gathered from ten major automotive subcontractor companies inhabiting the same supply chain cycle, exhibiting diverse degrees of lean implementations. What makes these datasets special is their ability to reveal the key features in evaluating the comparative impacts of lean tools, such as just-in-time (JIT), Single-Minute Exchange of Die (SMED), and Kaizen, to measure operational performance. These datasets are harnessed using the Bayesian technique to reveal the general characteristics of the models when used standalone and, most importantly, their possible integration into one another to achieve further improvements in productivity and flexibility. The results suggest that some lean tools may enhance productivity when used standalone, but limit flexibility in complex production environments. On the other hand, particular combinations of them (e.g., JIT + SMED + Kaizen) lead to cues in addressing the productivity–flexibility tradeoffs and synergistic benefits, specifically in increasing productivity with improved flexibility in dynamic production cycles. The findings in this work not only enrich the literature, considering the innovative suggestions with a data-driven, multifaceted methodology in the management of operations, but also support the decision-makers/stakeholders to improve the crucial lean techniques in real-world nondeterministic manufacturing settings, using a closed-loop co-optimization production model, aiming to help transform Industry 4.0 into Industry 5.0 and Industry 6.0.
