TY  - JOUR
TI  - Circular manufacturing in sustainable production systems for building materials: A systematic scoping review of emerging pathways
AU  - Gina Cynthia Raphita Hasibuan
AU  - Rezky Ariessa Dewi
AU  - Gea Geby Aurora Syafridon
AU  - M. Thoriq Al Fath
AU  - Naeemah Yusof
PY  - 2026
T2  - Next Materials
DO  - 10.1016/j.nxmate.2026.103809
AB  - Circular manufacturing is gaining attention as the construction sector seeks to reduce resource extraction, construction and demolition waste, and environmental burdens from building material production. The current evidence, however, remains fragmented across material pathways, digital tools, assessment methods, and implementation barriers. A PRISMA based systematic scoping review was conducted using Scopus and Google Scholar for English language publications from 2015 to 2026. The review synthesized evidence on waste streams, circular manufacturing strategies, building material applications, environmental assessment, technical performance, digital tools, challenges, and future research needs. The findings indicate that circular manufacturing pathways may enable sustainable production of building materials, as long as feedstock quality, processing conditions, substitution rates, and the contexts in which materials are applied are rigorously managed. Alkali activated and geopolymer systems, recycled aggregate concrete, waste glass and ceramic based materials, bio-based composites, and polymer based products show potential to reduce reliance on virgin resources while maintaining functional material performance in selected applications. Digital and analytical tools, including LCA, BIM, GIS, digital twins, machine learning, sensor monitoring, and material characterization, can support environmental assessment, traceability, performance prediction, and decision making. Nevertheless, adoption remains constrained by material variability, incomplete LCA comparability, limited external validation of predictive models, durability uncertainty, leaching and safety concerns, fragmented standards, and weak integration between research evidence and industrial practice. Future studies should strengthen comparable LCA protocols, performance based standards, leaching and durability verification, externally validated predictive models, interoperable circular material data systems, and cross-sector collaboration. These priorities are essential for moving circular manufacturing from isolated material feasibility toward reliable and scalable construction practice.
