TI  - Optimizing Additive Manufacturing: Artificial Intelligence-Driven Process Innovations for Advanced Industrial and Energy Systems
AU  - ASM Zulkarnayeen
PY  - 2025
T2  - Frontora
DO  - 10.70945/f.v03i02.0029
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) are shaping the future of additive manufacturing (AM) as it continues to revolutionize the manufacturing industry. Artificial Intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) are revolutionizing the manufacturing industry, transforming the landscape of Additive Manufacturing (AM). This review investigates process innovations based on AI to enhance quality, operations in advanced manufacturing systems, as well as energy performance and sustainability in energy sector systems. Real time process monitoring, adaptive control, defect detection, microstructure prediction, and predictive maintenance are highlighted as application areas of AI to laser powder bed fusion and other metal AM technologies. Optimal processing conditions and component performance prediction are discussed and computer vision, multimodal sensor fusion, physics informed learning, Bayesian optimization and surrogate modeling are highlighted as approaches to identify optimal processing conditions and predict component performance. The possibility of using digital twins and closed loop control for supporting the design optimization, autonomous manufacturing and quality assurance is also explored. The applications, such as those in the aerospace, automotive, medical, and energy sectors, point to new ways to improve component reliability, minimize material waste, improve energy use, and gain speed in developing advanced functional materials. However, there are many issues to be addressed, such as the lack of training datasets, inconsistencies in defect annotation, lack of cross machine and cross material validation, and absence of benchmarking frameworks. Overall, AI and AM are a powerful combination with significant promise for the development of reliable, energy-efficient, and sustainable industrial processes.
