TI  - Enterprise Digital Risk Twin Architecture for Predictive Governance Using Artificial Intelligence: A Design-Science Framework
AU  - Harshendra Gite
PY  - 2025
T2  - 
DO  - 10.52710/jisem.v10i61s.15330
AB  - The rapid expansion of cloud computing, artificial intelligence, cyber-physical systems, digital supply chains, Internet of Things technologies, enterprise platforms, and interconnected third-party services has transformed the nature of organizational risk. Traditional enterprise risk-management systems rely heavily on periodic assessment, static risk registers, historical indicators, and manually constructed dashboards. Although such mechanisms remain valuable for governance and compliance, they are often insufficient for anticipating rapidly evolving, nonlinear, interconnected, and cascading risk events. This paper proposes an Enterprise Digital Risk Twin architecture for predictive governance using artificial intelligence. The Enterprise Digital Risk Twin is conceptualized as a continuously synchronized digital representation of organizational risk conditions, enterprise dependencies, controls, predictive indicators, and governance decisions. Drawing on digital-twin theory, enterprise risk management, artificial intelligence governance, cybersecurity, data governance, explainable artificial intelligence, and design-science research, the study develops a seven-layer architecture encompassing enterprise observation, semantic and data integration, risk-state modeling, predictive intelligence, scenario simulation, governance orchestration, and assurance. Mathematical formulations are proposed for dynamic risk forecasting, dependency-driven risk propagation, uncertainty estimation, and governance-action selection. The proposed framework integrates machine learning, graph analytics, simulation, explainable artificial intelligence, organizational policies, risk appetite, and human oversight within a continuous predictive-governance cycle. The study does not claim empirical implementation results but provides a structured evaluation framework involving cyber intrusion, cloud-service disruption, supplier failure, artificial-intelligence model degradation, compliance failure, and multi-risk cascading scenarios. The paper contributes to the literature by extending digital-twin concepts from physical and manufacturing systems to enterprise-wide risk governance and by positioning predictive governance as a closed-loop process combining observation, prediction, simulation, explanation, decision-making, intervention, and organizational learning. The proposed architecture provides a theoretical and practical foundation for organizations seeking to transition from reactive risk management toward continuous and predictive enterprise governance.
