AU  - Урал Эдгарович Аллаяров
AU  - Елизавета Сергеевна Макшанцева
AU  - Дарья Павловна Дурнева
PY  - 2026
T2  - Oil and Gas Business
DO  - 10.17122/ogbus-2026-5-119-147
AB  - This article investigates the challenges of developing a comprehensive quantitative assessment of industrial safety in a modern industrial production characterized by a high degree of technological connectivity, multi-level management decisions, and the increasing role of the human factor. It is shown that traditional safety management methods – calculating the probability of hazardous events, assessing the severity of consequences, monitoring key performance indicators, and modeling the reliability of technical devices – are typically used independently, without the formation of a unified calculation framework. This separation does not provide a systematic understanding of the state of an enterprise, since the level of safety in real-world conditions is determined by the simultaneous action of technical, organizational, personnel, and economic factors, and emergency situations most often arise from a combination of these factors, rather than an isolated failure or violation. This paper analyzes existing approaches to assessing industrial safety. The article considers matrix models of professional risk, in which the danger level is determined by a combination of the probability of an event and the severity of consequences; point-rating methods that allow the aggregation of heterogeneous enterprise performance indicators; probabilistic models of equipment reliability management that make it possible to predict failures and optimize periods between repairs; As well as fuzzy logic methods used to formalize qualitative characteristics, expert assessments, and incomplete source information. It was established that each of these tools provides useful but fragmented information, whereas effective management requires their unification into a single mathematical construct. Based on the analysis, a system of indicators was developed, structured according to four functional areas: personnel (qualifications, turnover, length of service, frequency of briefings), technical (reliability, failure rate, wear of fixed assets, effectiveness of protective devices), organizational (availability of regulations, discipline, frequency of inspections), and economic (labor protection costs, return on assets, share of safety expenses in cost). Each indicator is reduced to a dimensionless form by means of normalization, which ensures their comparability. The weighting coefficients reflecting the importance of individual groups of factors are determined by expert analysis. A mathematical model of an integrated assessment of industrial safety has been developed, in which individual indicators are aggregated using additive convolution, and the final assessment is formed using a fuzzy composition approach. This approach allows for the consideration of nonlinear relationships between parameters and the uncertainty inherent in collecting data in real-world production conditions. An algorithm for calculating the integrated safety index is proposed, including sequential processing of the initial data, the formation of an accessory matrix, and a defuzzification procedure to convert qualitative characteristics to a quantitative indicator. The resulting integrated index can be used to monitor the dynamics of the enterprise's condition, comparatively evaluate various departments, identify problem areas, and substantiate management decisions. Including equipment reliability parameters and personnel training levels in the model increases the accuracy of the final assessment and enables tracking changes in the production environment over time. The model creates a methodological basis for automating industrial safety management procedures, can be adapted to specific industries, and serves as a foundation for the further development of predictive tools, including the use of machine learning methods and digital twins of production systems.
KW  - SLR-S1
N1  - OpenAlex W7225315164
