TY  - JOUR
TI  - Toolpath Ecologies
AU  - Eugenio Bettucchi
AU  - Francesco Polvi
PY  - 2026
T2  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - 10.5281/zenodo.22777682
AB  - Toolpath Ecologies Workshop Aim "Toolpath Ecologies" sets out to investigate and expand the knowledge around non-planar 3D printing, with a specific focus on conformal deposition at architectural scale. Rather than treating large-scale additive manufacturing as a means of reproducing predetermined digital geometry, the workshop framed it as a design tool for spatial articulation, merging computational strategy and experimental, hands-on making. Most non-planar printing research still treats the mold, the surface, or the substrate as fixed, and asks the toolpath to adapt to it. This workshop inverted that relationship, asking what happens to design thinking once the base geometry itself becomes reconfigurable and the toolpath is generated and adapted in direct response to it, on or off site. The workshop was conceived and delivered as one of the opening events of ROB|ARCH 2026, hosted by Robots in Architecture at Arkitektskolen Aarhus, and was facilitated by two practitioners with parallel backgrounds in computational design and robotic fabrication, spanning both industrial and academic contexts. The hardware set up was composed of one ABB4600 robotic arm, a RealSense L515 scanner and the WASP XL extruder. Workflow: From Scan to Print The workshop's methodological core is the introduction of double-curved, configurable molds as the base geometry for deposition, paired with an entry-level 3D scanner used to capture the physical state of that mold at each iteration. This combination is what allows the workflow to bridge the digital and physical realms directly: rather than fabricating toward a fixed model, participants design and produce components on top of a surface that can itself be reshaped between spatial variations, with the digital model updated to match. Participants began from a double curved global geometry, chosen because it could plausibly resemble a range of architectural elements, such as space dividers, interior claddings, variable-density false ceilings, etc. This global form was then subdivided, and each participant group took ownership of a portion of it, paired with a corresponding mold configuration built to approximate that portion's curvature. After each mold was configured and ready to be printed on, the scanning phase captured its actual geometry as a point cloud and brought it into Rhinoceros, producing a digital twin of the physical setup as it actually existed, rather than as it had been specified. This step is fundamental, as the scan closes the gap between intended and achieved geometry before printing begins. These calibration, scanning, and registration steps were coordinated through a dedicated app developed by the instructors, consolidating them into a single interface rather than a series of disconnected tools. The process in the app follows the workflow in steps, starting from the set up calibration, which defines the camera location in the robot spatial environment. The calibration is run via robot program, referencing the scans together with the robot positions. The app retains these extrinsic parameters for the next scanning sessions, making the robot-scan a unique system. Once the set up is finished, all the next captures are cleaned, merged and aligned. Localization follows the calibration: each cloud is scaled, transformed through the flange-to-camera relation and referred to the robot base frame, so that the mold is described in the same coordinate frame as the toolpath. Toolpath generation for each design iteration was then managed in Grasshopper, drawing on the digital mesh extracted from the scanned point cloud. Two checks were built into this stage: an error check comparing the incoming scanned point cloud against the intended design surface, and a printing simulation used to verify feasibility before any material was deposited. The final stage of the workflow is arguably where the pedagogical and the architectural arguments meet most directly. Because every group's printed element was designed against a shared global geometry, the individual pieces were able to connect one another at their contact points once removed from their molds. Assembled together, they produced a coherent spatial language emerging from multiple independent interventions: overlaps, depth, and varying degrees of visual and physical permeability all appeared as consequences of the shared design logic, not as something separately choreographed. Industry Potential Positioned at the intersection of current 3D printing research and architectural practice, the workshop's central claim is that conformal printing can act as a catalyst for spatial and geometrical expressions that planar, mold-independent printing forecloses. This has direct bearing on how large-scale additive manufacturing might be adopted in practice, beyond the research context in which it is currently most visible. The choice of test geometries, space dividers, interior claddings, variable-density ceilings, was deliberate: these are all building components for which the industry already accepts a degree of formal customization, but for which current production methods (CNC-milled molds, hand-built formwork, or panelized planar printing) impose a real cost penalty on that customization. A workflow in which the mold is reconfigurable and the toolpath is generated against a scan of its actual state removes a substantial part of that penalty. It converts formal variation from a cost driver into a design variable, which is precisely the condition under which architects and fabricators are able to specify curvature and complexity without treating it as a premium feature reserved for signature projects. There is a second, less obvious industry implication in the workflow's treatment of tolerance. Reconfigurable molds are, by construction, imprecise relative to fixed formwork, and a production pipeline that could not absorb that imprecision would be commercially uninteresting regardless of its design potential. By scanning the mold before every print rather than assuming it matches specification, the workflow treats geometric deviation as an input to be designed around rather than a defect to be eliminated. This is a meaningfully different posture from most current non-planar printing research, and one that maps more directly onto the tolerances a fabrication shop actually encounters on site, where formwork shifts, molds deform under load, and substrates are rarely as flat or as fixed as a digital model assumes. Finally, the involvement of a common industrial extruder situates the workshop's findings within a commercially available hardware context rather than a bespoke research rig. This matters for industry uptake: a workflow that depends on hardware only a handful of research labs possess has a very different adoption path than one built around a pellet extruder already in circulation in professional and educational fabrication settings. The same logic extends to the software layer: the interface is not tied to a single scanner model or robotic language, which further widens the range of existing fabrication setups it could realistically be adopted into. Findings The most consistent finding to emerge from running the workshop concerns the ease with which the workflow allowed movement between conformal printing strategies and between distinct fabrication workflows, without requiring participants to restart their design thinking each time the physical conditions changed. This showed up most clearly in the transition between mold configurations. Because the toolpath was never generated against an assumed, idealized surface but against a freshly scanned digital twin of whatever mold configuration a group was actually working with, switching from one mold geometry to another did not require a new toolpath strategy so much as a re-evaluation of the same strategy against new input data. In practice, this meant that groups could move from one portion of the global geometry to another, or revise a mold's curvature after seeing a first print, without the kind of workflow disruption that typically accompanies a change in base geometry in more conventional non-planar printing setups, where toolpath logic is often bound tightly to a specific, fixed surface. The same ease of transition was visible in how the workshop handled the relationship between global design intent and local physical conditions. Because the underlying logic explicitly detangles form from path, deviations captured during scanning — whether from mold assembly tolerance or intentional reconfiguration between iterations — were absorbed at the level of toolpath generation rather than propagating back into a redesign of the global geometry. Participants could therefore treat local adaptation and global coherence as compatible rather than competing goals: the printed outcome adapted to whatever the mold actually presented, while the global design intention, and consequently the way each piece would eventually register against its neighbors, remained stable throughout. This translated into a genuinely low barrier to entry for participants without a strong background in robotic toolpa
