TY  - JOUR
TI  - Digital Twin-Driven Framework for Automated Generation of Digital Product Passports
AU  - Xiangyu Jiang
AU  - Vitasta Jain
AU  - Swarit Anand Singh
AU  - Mark Johnson
AU  - Saeed Farahani
PY  - 2026
T2  - Manufacturing Letters
DO  - 10.1016/j.mfglet.2026.08.077
AB  - Digital Product Passport (DPP) emerges as a critical tool to address challenges in product traceability, circularity, and sustainability. It provides detailed, reliable product information to track materials, enable reuse, and optimize recycling. However, current approaches to generating DPPs rely on fragmented data sources, resulting in inconsistent information and reduced reliability. There is a need for an automated framework that can efficiently capture and manage product data to create its DPP. This work proposes an innovative framework that leverages digital twin (DT) technology, a key enabler of Industry 4.0, to automate the generation of DPPs. The proposed framework establishes a dynamic, bidirectional connection between a physical manufacturing system and its digital model. This connection enables the integration of material information, manufacturing process data, energy and resource consumption, and end-of-life (EOL) considerations into a single verifiable digital record for each manufactured product. The resulting DPP accurately reflects the product information required for regulatory compliance and life cycle assessment (LCA)-based sustainability evaluation. The architectural components of this framework include data acquisition, lifecycle modeling, and automated DPP generation through DT. A laboratory-scale conceptual case study of an injection-molded composite battery enclosure demonstrates the potential of a DT-driven DPP generation framework. It illustrates the feasibility of capturing manufacturing process data and integrating a DT model with LCA to automatically generate required information for the DPP. This approach streamlines compliance with forthcoming regulations and provides key insights for sustainable product design and manufacturing. © 2026 The Authors. Published by ELSEVIER Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0) Peer-review under responsibility of the scientific committee of the NAMRI|SME.
