TY  - JOUR
TI  - Policy-Guarded Explainable ML–LLM Framework for Data-Driven Control in Digital Twin Surrogate
AU  - Anuj Gautam
AU  - Manish Raj Aryal
AU  - Sam Anand
PY  - 2026
T2  - Manufacturing Letters
DO  - 10.1016/j.mfglet.2026.08.051
AB  - Recently, digital twins (DTs) have been increasingly used in manufacturing for predictive maintenance, adaptive control, and system-level optimization. The application of artificial intelligence (AI) to control machine operations through DTs is rapidly expanding as AI can identify complex patterns and make adaptive decisions on the fly. However, the scarcity of high-quality time-series data remains a critical bottleneck for training AI systems. Moreover, the use of unverified AI control strategies directly on live machines poses significant safety and reliability risks. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a data-driven surrogate framework, which serves as a virtual replica of the physical machine for safe experimentation. A conditional variational autoencoder (CVAE) was employed to generate realistic time-series data for manufacturing processes with Soft-Dynamic Time Warping (soft-DTW) as an additional loss objective. A machine-learning (ML) layer performs anomaly detection and process prediction while a Large Language Model (LLM) interprets system states, explains detected anomalies, and recommends corrective actions. The framework also includes a policy guard module, which validates these recommendations against predefined operational and safety constraints to ensure reliability and rule compliance before execution. The proposed framework is scalable and is validated using multiple surrogates of CNC milling machines and FDM 3D printers. This surrogate-based case study demonstrates that policy-guarded AI integration within a digital twin environment enables safe, reproducible, and scalable research toward intelligent and autonomous manufacturing systems. © 2026 The Authors. Published by ELSEVIER Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0) Peer-review under responsibility of the scientific committee of the NAMRI|SME.
