TY  - JOUR
TI  - Integrating 5G-enabled mobile robotics for remote supervision in CNC machining via a virtualized framework
AU  - Osman Bodur
AU  - Tanmay Badhan
AU  - Neslihan Demir
AU  - Sri Sai Malik Karthikeya Varma Bhupathiraju
AU  - Stefan Spettel
AU  - Friedrich Bleicher
PY  - 2026
T2  - CIRP journal of manufacturing science and technology
DO  - 10.1016/j.cirpj.2026.10.008
AB  - Modern manufacturing demands flexible automation to handle variable tasks, yet many legacy machines lack digital interfaces and require onsite human presence to manage exceptions and deviations. This work presents a virtualized supervision framework that integrates autonomous mobile robots with manipulators, digital twins and private 5G networks to bridge this gap. Unlike existing 5G-enabled robot systems, which target digitally native equipment and separate network from robot control, the framework addresses legacy CNC centers with physical-only interfaces, conditions operator authority on network and positioning confidence, and validates control strategies in a Virtual 5G Lab beforehand. Routine operations run autonomously, while exceptions are escalated to a remote operator through a Remote-Control Centre combining live production data, simulation feedback, and safety policies. A digital twin environment implemented in Ignition Gazebo enables a simulation-first workflow verifying operator actions before physical execution. Deployed over a private standalone 5G network, the framework achieves a median end-to-end round-trip time of 6–9 ms, within the 10–20 ms bound required for safe manipulator jogging near humans and fixtures, with downlink and uplink throughput of 0.98 Gb/s and 0.12–0.20 Gb/s. Rel-16 5G positioning achieves median accuracy of 1.5–2.0 m (LoS) and 2.5–3.5 m (NLoS), sufficient to gate teleoperation authority, while the tighter tolerance for vision-guided tool exchange is met by an eye-in-hand RGB-D sensor. The framework is demonstrated and experimentally evaluated in a CNC machining cell, enabling automated tool handoff, machine tending, and supervised in-cell inspection using an autonomous mobile robot.
