TY  - JOUR
TI  - A Comprehensive Architectural, Cryptographic, and Cyber-Physical Analysis of Blockchain-Secured Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) and Digital Twin Synchronization in Industry 4.0 Smart Manufacturing: From PUF Zero-Trust Authentication to Predictive Maintenance and Cloud Manufacturing-as-a-Service
AU  - Priyanshu Raj, Dheeraj Yadav, Dr. Vishal Shrivastava, Dr.Ashok Kajla, Mr.Rahul Sharma,  Er.Ram Babu Buri
PY  - 2026
T2  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - 10.5281/zenodo.23996814
AB  - The convergence of Operational Technology (OT) and Information Technology (IT) under the Industry 4.0 paradigm has transformed traditional isolated factories into hyper-connected Cyber-Physical Production Systems (CPPS). In modern smart manufacturing, Computer Numerical Control (CNC) machining centers, 6-DOF industrial robotic arms, Additive Manufacturing (3D printing) cells, and Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs) stream continuous high-frequency telemetry via the Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) to synchronize real-time virtual replicas known as Industrial Digital Twins (IDTs). Concurrently, Cloud Manufacturing-as-a-Service (CMaaS) platforms enable original equipment manufacturers (OEMs) to outsource on-demand part production across globally distributed micro-factories. However, legacy centralized Manufacturing Execution Systems (MES) and cloud-hosted Digital Twin repositories suffer from single-point-of-failure downtime risks, Stuxnet-style PLC command spoofing, counterfeit sensor firmware injection, intellectual property (IP) theft of proprietary 3D CAD/G-code files, and opaque Service-Level Agreement (SLA) disputes during unplanned machine breakdowns. Blockchain technology—combining tamper-evident distributed ledgers, low-latency industrial consensus, and deterministic smart contracts—provides a decentralized Zero-Trust security fabric for smart factories. This paper presents a comprehensive architectural, mathematical, and cyber-physical investigation of blockchain technology in Industry 4.0 IIoT and Digital Twin ecosystems. We formalize a five-layer industrial reference architecture integrating SRAM Physical Unclonable Function (PUF) shop-floor devices, IEEE 802.1Qbv Time-Sensitive Networking (TSN) and OPC UA edge gateways, hybrid Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) and Credit-Raft consortium ledgers, InterPlanetary File System (IPFS) CAD storage, and automated smart contracts. We formulate rigorous mathematical models for PUF-anchored challengeresponse device authentication, cryptographic Digital Twin state-synchronization divergence bounds, Wiener stochastic bearing degradation and Remaining Useful Life (RUL) smart contract maintenance triggers, and gametheoretic CMaaS capacity matching. Finally, we benchmark authentication overhead on ARM Cortex-M4 controllers, Digital Twin sync latency across 2,000 sensors, and industrial operational KPI improvements.
