AB  - Modern manufacturing systems are increasingly adopting automation technologies to improve production efficiency, operational flexibility, and material handling performance. This study evaluates how different levels of automation influence manufacturing system behavior within a mixed-model production environment using discrete-event simulation (DES). Three production configurations representing manual, semi-automated, and fully automated operations were developed and investigated under identical production requirements and operational conditions. The production system consists of six processing machines, manual workstations, conveyor systems, Automated Guided Vehicles (AGVs), and robotic handling technologies. To assess system robustness under uncertainty, a structured sensitivity analysis based on a 3 × 3 full-factorial design was implemented using variations in Process Time (PT) and Mean Time To Repair (MTTR). The simulation experiments analyzed the effect of automation level and operational variability primarily using throughput and mean lead time as system-level performance indicators. Results showed that all three scenarios had relatively stable behavior with limited sensitivity to PT and MTTR variations. Throughput and lead time remained largely unchanged across the different automation configurations, indicating that increasing automation did not materially improve productivity within the studied production layout. The findings suggest that the effectiveness of automation technologies strongly depends on system complexity, routing characteristics, and material handling requirements. In relatively simple and stable production environments, advanced automation may provide limited operational benefits compared to manual or semi-automated systems.
