AB  - Digital twins are now routinely proposed as the integrating layer of connected manufacturing, yet the three functions they are expected to serve state synchronization, predictive maintenance and dynamic workload balancing have been specified in separate methodological traditions that rarely share a formalism. This article diagnoses the resulting deficiency as a methodological gap rather than a knowledge or empirical gap, and develops a synthesis oriented toward its closure. Through a systematic review and framework synthesis, the study maps the relevant literature onto a three-layer architecture mirroring, prognostics and allocation and formalizes the interfaces between them. Four findings emerge. First, the digital twin literature itself presents synchronization as its constitutive and least standardized problem, with difficulties arising from variability, uncertainty, mismatched scales between physical and virtual spaces and continuously generated multi-entity data, and with no standard field-level mechanism available in manufacturing systems. Second, the prognostics literature documents that machine learning for predictive maintenance is far more successful on laboratory and reference datasets than in real-world industrial conditions, while remaining useful life prediction lacks an explicit framework for general process analysis, and the black-box character of deep learning models limits real-world application while engineers remain reluctant to act on predictions they cannot comprehend. Third, the scheduling literature achieves strong dynamic performance through reinforcement learning but identifies scalability, interpretability, data availability and the standardization of performance metrics as unresolved and reports that simulation-based distributed scheduling is still largely implemented with historical data and empirical rules in a static manner. Fourth, the interfaces that would couple the three layers are precisely the ones left unformalized : no reviewed method treats twin fidelity as an explicit variable in the maintenance-scheduling objective. The article formalizes a synchronization-aware maintenance-scheduling problem in which fidelity constrains the admissibility of prognoses, and formulates five propositions for its validation.
