TY  - JOUR
TI  - Digital Twin Orchetration System For Industrial Manufacturing
AU  - Agam Ramteke
AU  - Pranay Tarjule
AU  - Pritam Kumar Rai
AU  - Jafar Ansari
PY  - 2026
T2  - INTERANTIONAL JOURNAL OF SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH IN ENGINEERING AND MANAGEMENT
DO  - 10.55041/ijsrem68224
AB  - Digital twin platforms for discrete manufacturing are dominated by managed cloud offerings (Azure Digital Twins, AWS IoT TwinMaker, Siemens Xcelerator, PTC ThingWorx) and by a growing but fragmented set of open-source, Kubernetes-native frameworks (Eclipse Ditto, KTWIN, OpenTwins, IndustryFusion, OFacT). Two open questions motivate this work. First, whether a self-hosted, Kubernetes-orchestrated architecture can support multiple synchronized machine twins for a discrete production line — modeled with causal, empirically informed simulators rather than randomized sensor generators — while remaining operable on commodity infrastructure realistic for an SME. Second, whether representing process-level dependencies between independently deployed machine twins yields decision-support value beyond isolated per-machine monitoring. This paper specifies a five-station automotive-component line (Stamping → CNC → Weld/Assembly → Quality Inspection → Packaging/Dispatch), a Live-Twin/Scenario-Worker architectural split with a DAG-based process twin under frozen-snapshot semantics, reduced-order causal machine models calibrated against publicly available industrial datasets (PHM Society 2010 Milling, NASA IMS Bearing, AI4I 2020, Bosch Production Line Performance), and a full experimental protocol for synchronization, scalability, resilience, and model-fidelity evaluation. The related-work analysis situates the contribution against both managed platforms and the Kubernetes-digital-twin literature, with particular care to distinguish digital twins that run on Kubernetes (this work; KTWIN) from digital twins of Kubernetes itself (KAPETÁNIOS, KubeTwin) — a distinction the existing literature does not always make explicit. Quantitative claims about synchronization latency, scalability limits, and simulator fidelity are deferred to the experimental sections and marked pending until measured on the deployed system.
