TY  - JOUR
TI  - International and Home News
AU  - Melanie Brown
PY  - 2022
T2  - Food Science and Technology
DO  - 10.1002/fsat.3604_2.x
AB  - Our theme for December is food education and citizen science. As warnings about climate change from COP27 become more stark, it is increasingly important to communicate with citizens about the impact of food on health, food security, sustainability, the environment and climate change. There will be some hard choices. Citizen science, or scientific research conducted with public participation, has proved to be an important way of understanding public attitudes as well as informing the public about food safety, healthy diets and food sustainability. It has also provided feedback on the value of potential targeted messages and interventions from regulatory authorities. This suggests that when people feel they are part of the process, they are more likely to be engaged and willing to change their behaviour (p28). Offering citizens the opportunity to learn about healthy, sustainable diets, for example by attending healthy cooking courses (p40), is another approach to educating people about good food choices. Taking part in research projects, such as investigating chopping board microbiology, can both educate the public and also provide large scale data on food-related issues in the home (p32). Research has focused on finding the most effective way of communicating with different groups within the population (p28). It is important for communications to be clear; a lack of consensus among experts can lead to mixed messages, for example about ultra-processed foods (p36). Innovative approaches, such as storytelling and artworks, can be effective ways of helping to interpret research data for the public as well as getting important messages across (p20). I would like to wish readers a Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year. As this is my last issue as editor of FS&T, I would also like to thank the Advisory Panel, IFST staff and all IFST members who have supported me and contributed ideas and articles to FS&T over the years. I wish the new editor, Veronica Giacintucci, every success. email [email protected] Precision agriculture specialists, Agri-EPI Centre, AI-driven autonomous robotics company Antobot and vineyard owner, Ian Beecher-Jones, have embarked on two projects at JoJo's vineyard near Henley-on-Thames to create a digital map and on-the-ground and aerial monitoring1. The digital infrastructure project – funded by Innovate UK and Defra as part of their Farm Innovation Programme Research Starter Round 2 – will create the digital infrastructure of the vineyard, including rows, posts and vines to an accuracy of two centimetres using real time kinetic (RTK) surveying tools. The shareable infrastructure model, based on the Australian Collabriculture project could save producers many hours of work and cost in setting their vineyards up ready to embrace viticultural technology. The digital-infrastructure map aims to provide a common understanding and ‘language’ for both growers and ag-tech providers ensuring that precision can be matched to reality. It should help to address the labour shortages the industry is facing by allowing a more accessible way for vineyards to embrace robotics and AI technology. On-the-ground and aerial monitoring will be gathered by robots and drones to add a layer of data to the digital map. The robots are being developed by agriculture robot technologists, Antobot, and drones are supplied by Agri-EPI Centre. Antobot will use its various robot applications during the project, such as logistics (Assist) and scouting (Insight), to ensure that the digital-infrastructure captures multiple use-case requirements and is robust in a variety of tasks and conditions. This second strand of work is funded by the European Institute of Innovation and Technology (EIT). Creating a shareable digital twin of the vineyard should cut down the amount of time that contractors spend out in the field, saving producers and technology companies time and money. If growers have their own digital infrastructure built to a standardised format, it can be shared with any technology company the grower would like to work with, reducing duplication of unnecessary onboarding, and set up time every time a new technology is to be tested and tried in the vineyard. The resulting technology should be highly transferable to other row crop sectors, such as orchards and soft fruit. The first comprehensive assessment of the presence of organophosphate esters (OPEs) in UK foods has been carried out by researchers at the University of Birmingham and published in Science of the Total Environment2. Organophosphates – chemicals used as flame retardants in furnishings and textiles, building, food packaging materials and decorating materials, as well in various other consumer products – are toxic to human health at high levels, or with long term exposure3 and their use is increasing worldwide. They can be ingested from dust or from air. Nearly 400 food samples were tested for evidence of OPEs. The team divided product samples into 15 food groups that were either animal-derived or plant-derived and tested for eight different OPEs. They found concentrations were highest in milk and milk products, followed by those in cereal and cereal products. Concentrations were lowest in chickens’ eggs. The chemicals triphenyl phosphate (TPHP) and 2-ethylhexyl diphenyl phosphate (EHDPP) were most common, being found in all food samples except egg and egg products. Overall, the concentrations in animal-derived foods were statistically indistinguishable from those found in foods derived from plants. The team also estimated daily dietary intakes across four age groups: toddlers, children, elderly people and adults. Baby food contributed 39% of OPE intake for toddlers, while non-alcoholic beverages were the main contributor for children (27%). In adults and the elderly, cereal products (25%) and fruit (22%) were the main contributors. While the levels found in all samples were below those currently deemed to be a risk to health, the researchers suggest that these chemicals can build up in the body's fatty tissues over time. For children and toddlers the safety margins were much narrower under high-end exposure scenarios for some OPEs, specifically: EHDPP, tris(2-butoxyethyl) phosphate (TBOEP, tris(2-chloroisopropyl) phosphate (TCIPP) and tris(1,3-dichloro-2-propyl) phosphate (TDCIPP). The researchers conclude that food is a significant source of human exposure to OPEs in the UK and that more work is needed to fully understand the risks of continuing to increase our use of these chemicals. They suggest that this baseline survey should be a wake-up call to industrial users of OPEs to start exploring alternatives. Food producers are encouraged to investigate supply chains to better understand where contaminants might be introduced. The Food Standards Agency (FSA) has published a report analysing 20 years of data on antimicrobial resistance (AMR) in Campylobacter from retail chicken in the UK4. The study aimed to assess any trends during this 20-year period and provides a baseline against which to evaluate future changes in AMR. AMR can develop in any bacterium, including Campylobacter, which is the main cause of bacterial food poisoning in the developed world and it is estimated that there are in excess of half a million cases annually in the UK. The FSA has commissioned several UK-wide retail surveys and sampling studies that involved testing for Campylobacter in chicken. A significant proportion of the Campylobacter isolates detected were further tested to assess resistance to a range of antimicrobials. Key findings from this report vary between the five main types of antimicrobial drugs included in the study. Resistance to quinolones (ciprofloxacin and nalidixic acid) and tetracycline was common in the most prevalent types of Campylobacter from chicken (Campylobacter jejuni and Campylobacter coli). In comparison, resistance to erythromycin and streptomycin was much rarer in the Campylobacter isolates examined. Gentamicin resistance was very rare. Dear Editor I read with interest the article by Beena Sharma on microalgae in the September issue of Food Science and Technology. In 1963 I was a research biologist on a WHO contract monitoring the growth of microalgae in man-made ponds in Zambia. One of the objectives was to use them as a food source for fish (e.g. tilapia) to aid the diet of the local villagers. When my contract expired in 1965 I returned to the UK and was offered a post with Unilever Research to lead a team investigating the potential of microalgae as a human food source. This was following an alarming UN report on ‘the protein gap’, which was emerging with the rapidly increasing world population. Unilever was one of the multinational food companies that agreed to fund research in this area with the focus on algae. RHM also committed to a similar project on fungi, later to result in Quorn. We started the project in June 1965 and our screening resulted in us selecting Chlorella as the primary species. After a series of successful and encouraging trials we began to scale up, and just over a year later we were able to produce large kilo quantities of dried Chlorella powder. The next challenge was to extract the protein and we achieved this during the second half of 1966. We were able to obtain kilogram amounts, enough to allow us to investigate texturising to get towards the target of an animal protein texture. Sadly, in 1967 a rework of the data used for the UN report showed that it had been the subject of a rather too enthusiastic interpretation and the situation was not as serious as first thought. At the next budget review Unilever downgraded the project and severely reduced the funding. We were forced to tie up the loose ends, write a final report (kept confidential by Unilever) and close down in the second half of 1967. It has taken five decades to catch up and carry on from where my team left off. I think we can claim to be the first to make textured plant protein from Chlorella. Peter Berry Ottaway CSci, FIFST Researchers in Scotland at Roslin Technologies and the University of Edinburgh, with support from the Industrial Biotechnology Innovation Centre (IBioIC), have demonstrated the cost-effective scale-up of pig cell lines for cultivated – or lab-grown – meat production, addressing key challenges for the industry in the UK5. They have developed a new approach to cell generation that eliminates variations between how different batches of cells grow. This has also cut the cost of cell culture media by 61% and could be scaled for use in industry-sized bioreactors. The technology makes induced pluripotent stem cells from a small sample of animal tissue. These cells self-renew and can be used to grow different types of tissue, such as muscle and fat. To date, a critical bottleneck in the process has been developing a cost-effective way of growing cells at large scale. However, by refining and optimising the media used for cell generation, the researchers will now be able to increase production from a 500ml bioreactor to 5l and, eventually, up to 2,000l – a similar size to the equipment used for commercial scale production of cultivated meat. Although a nascent industry, the market for cultivated meat products is expected to reach £21.2bn ($25bn) by 2030 according to research from McKinsey & Company. The findings could be the first building block of creating a cultivated meat industry in the UK, subject to regulatory approval for the sector's products. Singapore became the first country in the world to approve the sale of cultivated meat in December 2020, with others expected to follow. The process of creating cultivated meat is described as a key way of decarbonising the global food production system as well as meeting the protein needs of a rapidly growing global population, without the need to raise animals for slaughter. Finnish foodtech company Solar Foods has been given regulatory approval by the Singapore Food Agency (SFA) for the sale of food products containing Solein, a novel protein grown with CO2 and electricity, in Singapore6. This follows submission of a novel food dossier on Solein for the SFA's consideration in September 2021. Solein is grown with minimal resources and without arable land; it can be produced in deserts, Arctic regions, cities, or even outer space (FS&T, June 20207). The company describes it as being disconnected from the limitations of traditional agriculture, with the potential to transform the sustainability, availability and transparency of our food and where it can be produced. Solar Foods plans to seek GRAS (Generally Recognized As Safe) status assessment for Solein soon in the United States, and applications for novel food authorisation have been filed in other key markets, such as the UK and the European Union. The company's first commercial-scale production facility is set to begin operations in Finland in 2024. Scientists from Unilever and Wageningen University have published new findings in the Frontiers in Nutrition journal, which indicate that processing the majority of soy products increases the protein's nutritional quality8. Soy is often-used as a meat alternative because of its high protein content and quality, relative to other plant-based ingredients. Before being used in plant-based protein-rich products, ingredients, such as soy, generally undergo several processing methods including soaking, grinding and heating to create soy milk, or dehulling and fat extraction to create soybean flour, and then removing soluble carbohydrates from this to create soy protein concentrate. Consumers have previously expressed concern that processed soy products (found in meat-free alternatives) are made less nutritious by processing. The researchers performed a quantitative review of in-vivo and in-vitro studies that assessed the indispensable amino acid (IAA) composition and digestibility of various soy products to obtain their digestibility indispensable amino acids scores (DIAAS) and protein digestibility corrected amino acid scores (PDCAAS). The study found that processing and postprocessing conditions can increase or decrease protein quality scores between soy product groups, indicating that differences may be attributed to various forms of post-processing (such as additional heat-treatment or moisture conditions). The majority of soy products were found to have high protein quality scores; DIAAS increased from tofu, soy flakes, soy hulls, soy flour, soy protein isolate, soybean, soybean meal, soy protein concentrate to soymilk with the highest DIAAS. Micro Meat, a Mexico-based start-up producing cultivated meat, and Orbital Assembly, a developer of space-based business parks with variable gravity, are collaborating to co-develop cultivated meat production systems in space9. Micro Meat will install its proprietary meat production equipment onboard Orbital Assembly's Pioneer-class™ space station to provide food for space station personnel. The Pioneer-class™ stations are the world's first and largest hybrid space stations for both work and play and will be the first free-flying, habitable, privately-operated facilities in orbit. The project will give Micro Meat the opportunity to enhance the efficiency of its production process and make it more of a scalable protein production system on Earth. Micro Meat's scalable platform is reported to cultivate meat with the same nutrient and taste profile as animal-based meat. The company's proprietary scaffold system allows the production of meat in greater yields using existing bioreactors. The scaffolds are made of 3D, biocompatible and biodegradable materials that are designed to support the growth of cells and tissues and provide an exponentially increasing surface area for adherent cell culture surfaces. 25-26 January 2023 WINTER CONFERENCE 2022 AND 23 - ARCHITECTURE OF FOOD: PROCESSING, STRUCTURE AND HEALTH Venue The Royal Society, London web nutritionsociety.org/events/winter-conference-202223-architecture-food-processing-structure-and-health 20-22 March 2023 IFE INTERNATIONAL FOOD AND DRINK EVENT Venue ExCel London web excel.london/whats-on/ife-international-food-drink-event-2023 24-26 April 2023 FOOD AND DRINK EXPO Venue NEC Birmingham web foodanddrinkexpo.co.uk/ 16–19 July 2023 IFT FOOD EXPO Venue Chicago, Illinois web iftevent.org/schedule/future-dates
KW  - SLR-S2
N1  - OpenAlex W4311061967
ER  - 

