AB  - In Industry 4.0, manufacturers increasingly use digital twins (DTs) to develop real-time representations of shop floors. However, the application of DTs in production planning remains limited. This paper proposes a DT-driven methodology for production planning that integrates planning engines, simulation, forecasting, and data-driven models. The decision model is formulated as a multi-echelon, multi-item capacitated lot-sizing problem (MMCLSP). The DT improves model fidelity by extracting key parameters and characterizing uncertainties from historical and real-time data. We extend the deterministic MMCLSP to a stochastic formulation for demand uncertainty, and, introduce a closed-loop mechanism that updates production lead times using simulation feedback. Two industrial case studies demonstrate that the proposed framework effectively integrates aggregate production planning with shop-floor execution. Compared with deterministic approaches, the stochastic model with data-driven lead times yields more effective and operationally feasible production plans, enhancing decision support for manufacturing systems.
