AB  - In the modern industrial ecosystem, characterized by increasing complexity, significant demand fluctuations, and supply chain disruptions, traditional production planning paradigms based on static and deterministic models have lost their effectiveness. This research aims to provide a comprehensive solution for transitioning from this situation by exploring the transformative role of Digital Twin technology as the core of intelligent and dynamic planning systems. Methodologically, this study employs a qualitative meta analysis following the seven step approach by Sandelowski and Barroso. The statistical population includes 150 credible scientific documents published between 2023 and 2025, screened through purposive sampling. Findings indicate that Digital Twins significantly enhance key performance indicators through mechanisms such as integrating deep learning with operations research, unified energy management, and logistics synchronization. Furthermore, comparing the findings with lean production theories reveals that Digital Twins address the inherent weaknesses of Just-in-Time (JIT) production systems by eliminating uncertainty. Ultimately, deploying this technology is regarded not merely as a technical upgrade but as a policy imperative for ensuring national resilience, and a framework for data standardization and computational infrastructure development is proposed.
