TI  - IMPROVING THE ECONOMIC EFFICIENCY OF INDUSTRIAL ENTERPRISES BASED ON SIMULATION MODELING AND MACHINE LEARNING METHODS
AU  - Ruslan T. Gallyamov
AU  - Marianna A. Charuyskaya
PY  - 2026
T2  - EKONOMIKA I UPRAVLENIE PROBLEMY RESHENIYA
DO  - 10.36871/ek.up.p.r.2026.09.08.007
AB  - Traditional approaches to the technological development of enterprises primarily focus on eliminating production constraints and increasing equipment utilization, which does not always yield a positive economic outcome. This study aims to develop a methodological approach to generating economically justified scenarios for the technological development of a production system by integrating simulation modeling and reinforcement learning. The research methodology includes discrete-event simulation, a hierarchical Markov decision process, and investment analysis. The proposed two-level model selects organizational and technical measures and determines scenario parameter values. Action masking based on the state of the production system is used to reduce the search space. The economic viability of the measures is incorporated directly into the reward function through net present value calculations. Training is performed using the Proximal Policy Optimization algorithm. The methodological approach was tested in the development of a technological development concept for a tool manufacturing facility comprising seven production sections. During training, 119 scenarios were generated, each consisting of five sequential measures. The results indicate that combining low-cost measures at operations downstream of a production bottleneck can yield greater economic benefits by reducing cascading downtime and blocking. Increasing work-in-process buffer capacity was found to be less economically efficient for short production cycles than reducing equipment recovery time. In the comparison presented, the proposed scenario involves an investment of RUB 320 million, a net present value of RUB 436 million over five years, and a payback period of 1.8 years. The practical significance of the study lies in developing a management decision-support tool that evaluates sequences of measures while accounting for production interdependencies and investment returns. The findings can inform technological development decisions under investment resource constraints.
