AB  - Modern industrial production systems rely on a variety of sensors and sensor systems to capture qualified data, automate and control processes and to secure product quality. Sensors are the key elements to generate real world data and to feed trustworthy data into digital models or digital twins. A large variety of applications require a consistent digital metrology domain. It is a complex and challenging task, especially for modern internet of things (IOT) based factory environments with numerous sensors distributed. A stringent chain of traceable calibration information is essential when calibrating sensors and providing calibration data in a machine readable and machine interpretable way for a trusted digital quality infrastructure. The GEMIMEG-II project used and qualified the Digital Calibration Certificate (DCC) for industrial applications. It introduced quality indicators (QoX) with information on the trust level of the respective data. Finally, a DCC sub-model for the Asset Administration Shell (AAS) and Digital Product Passport (DPP) was prepared. A future perspective concludes this paper.
