TY  - JOUR
TI  - Sensitivity Analysis of Staffing Strategies for Production and Maintenance Support in a Simulated Production System
AU  - Amal Abucar
AU  - Anuradha Pothalkar
AU  - Akshay Goyal
PY  - 2026
T2  - IFAC-PapersOnLine
DO  - 10.1016/j.ifacol.2026.10.014
AB  - Production system performance is influenced not only by machine reliability, but also by the availability of human support resources required to restore equipment after failures. When maintenance support is limited, machines may remain idle while waiting for assistance, increasing downtime and reducing system performance. This study develops a simplified 3D discrete-event simulation model to evaluate two staffing strategies in a production segment with shared maintenance demand. In Scenario 1, four versatile operators perform both production and maintenance-related tasks. In Scenario 2, two production operators are supported by one dedicated maintenance technician. A scenario-based sensitivity analysis was conducted by varying Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF) and Mean Time To Repair (MTTR) at three levels (-20%, 0%, and +20%) using a full-factorial design with replicated simulation runs. The scenarios were compared using mean lead time, average machine availability, and total repair workload across machines. Under the modeled staffing policies, Scenario 1 achieves lower mean lead times, higher machine availability, and lower repair-related workload across the tested reliability conditions. In contrast, Scenario 2 is more sensitive to changes in MTBF and MTTR because maintenance support is constrained by a single technician, creating a repair bottleneck when multiple machines require attention simultaneously. The findings demonstrate the importance of modelling maintenance support as a limited resource and show how discrete-event simulation can support staffing decisions during production system design.
