AB  - In the Industry 5.0 era, managing complex multi-source sensor data has become critical for manufacturing system design. Conventional methods face significant challenges in handling spatiotemporal correlations, data incompleteness, and external disturbances. This paper introduces an innovative digital twin (DT) framework inspired by the concept of digital cousins (DCs) to address these challenges. The proposed architecture enables continuous system monitoring and predictive analytics, even under partial sensor failure conditions. Our approach integrates two key components: spatiotemporal recovery through twin modeling and predictive analysis utilizing DCs. The twin modeling component prioritizes error minimization in monitoring systems affected by data attacks or sensor malfunctions. We develop a specialized twin model for nonlinear time-series data, achieving high-precision reconstruction of low-rank spatiotemporal information to enhance sparse sensing capabilities. Meanwhile, DCs perform semantic analysis on multivariate time-series data, strengthening critical feature representation through hierarchical abstraction. The framework synergistically combines twin models, DCs, and an iterative inference mechanism to create interactive prediction capabilities that dynamically adapt to missing data states. Extensive experiments conducted in large-scale industrial environments demonstrate that our proposed approach achieves outstanding recovery and predictive accuracy for monitoring systems.
